# The Google Chrome flags easi.run's browser (main.rs) and the KasmVNC baseline (bench/kasmvnc/launch.sh) share, one per
# line; a line starting with # is a comment. Each adds its own beside them: the Ozone platform, the window, the proxy.
--no-first-run
--no-default-browser-check
# WebGL on Mesa's llvmpipe (software, as on any Linux machine without a GPU driver): Chrome blocks software renderers
# unless told otherwise, and without WebGL a browser reads as a bot. Composition stays in software, into the
# shared-memory buffers the compositor reads.
--ignore-gpu-blocklist
--disable-gpu-compositing
--force-renderer-accessibility
--user-data-dir=/home/browser/profile
--disk-cache-dir=/tmp/cache
# easi.run's browser is marked as automated (navigator.webdriver) by its launcher's DevTools pipe, which it is not: a
# person or the agent drives it through input, as without the pipe, and sites' bot checks would otherwise refuse the
# person.
--disable-blink-features=AutomationControlled
--proxy-bypass-list=<-loopback>
--disable-background-networking
--disable-component-update
# No spare renderer kept warm for the next site: one page at a time does not need it, and it holds 7-9 MiB.
# ParallelDownloading would write a download out of order; the launcher streams it in order.
--disable-features=MediaRouter,Translate,SpareRendererForSitePerProcess,ParallelDownloading
# The profile's tmpfs reads as an ordinary disk (so pages see the usual storage quota); this bounds what one site may
# store to 192 MiB (three quarters of the pool), and the pool is evicted beyond 256 MiB. The browser's memory limit
# bounds the rest (docs/security.md).
--enable-features=StorageQuotaSettings:PoolSizeBytes/268435456
